Mild acute external otitis (swimmer's ear) with localized canal tenderness and no systemic signs
Minor Ailments and Upsets
Tragal/pinna traction pain, Auditory canal pruritus, Scanty clear otorrhea

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MAU-012
Reason for Visit: Acute, severe unilateral ear pain requiring urgent otoscopic examination to exclude mastoiditis or complicated otitis media.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage identified severe ear pain exacerbated by movement, with new-onset aural discharge. Urgent clinical evaluation was necessary prior to the next in-hours period to directly visualize the tympanic membrane and rule out acute mastoiditis or a ruptured suppurative otitis media, which carry risks of hearing loss or deep space infection.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: My ear is throbbing constantly and it hurts intensely if I touch it.
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with progressive localized ear pain evolving over the past 24 hours, heavily exacerbated by traction on the pinna or pressure on the tragus. Reports pruritus within the external auditory canal starting 48 hours ago after swimming. Notes a sensation of aural fullness and scanty clear or slightly cloudy otorrhea. Hearing is subjectively preserved. No systemic fever or chills.
Functional Status: Fully independent, sleep mildly disturbed by pain.
Social/Safety Concerns: Discomfort is causing significant agitation.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 128/82 | HR: 84 | Temp: 37.1 C | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Alert, distressed when the affected ear is manipulated.
Physical Exam: Otoscopic examination reveals mild erythematous swelling of the canal skin. Intact mobile tympanic membrane with normal landmarks visualized past the mild edema. Diagnostic triad confirmed: pain elicited by tragal/pinna traction, auditory canal pruritus, and scanty clear otorrhea. Normal hearing thresholds to gross testing. Absence of mastoid tip tenderness or post-auricular erythema.
Environmental Observations: Swimming gear drying in the bathroom.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient trialed oral paracetamol with minimal relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Mild acute external otitis (swimmer's ear) with localized canal tenderness and no systemic signs - Status: Stable
Differentials:

Acute Otitis Media - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Intact tympanic membrane with normal landmarks and lack of middle ear effusion.

Mastoiditis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Complete absence of mastoid tip tenderness, normal post-auricular skin, and no high-grade fever.

Malignant Otitis Externa - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of granulation tissue at the bony-cartilaginous junction and lack of immunocompromise or severe nocturnal pain out of proportion to exam.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient reported acute, severe otalgia mimicking middle ear pathology or potential early mastoiditis. Direct visualization of the tympanic membrane was clinically mandated to rule out deeper infectious spread capable of threatening auditory function or progressing to osteomyelitis. Evaluating the patient urgently after-hours allowed for a definitive diagnosis of external canal inflammation, facilitating prompt initiation of topical therapy and preventing unnecessary systemic antibiotic use or ED presentation.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescription for topical antibiotic/corticosteroid otic drops (e.g., ciprofloxacin/dexamethasone). Recommendation for oral NSAIDs for pain control.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instill prophylactic 50/50 rubbing alcohol and white vinegar drops immediately after swimming to dry the canal and restore an acidic environment, keeping the bottle directly in the swim bag as a cue.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): Use of earplugs during aquatic activities.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 7 days if symptoms fail to resolve.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate presentation to Emergency Department or call 000 if you develop sudden swelling behind the ear, high fever, facial weakness, or sudden profound hearing loss.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is localized to the external canal; treatment failure requires re-evaluation of the tympanic membrane.
Clinical Course: Pain should significantly improve within 48 to 72 hours of starting the topical drops.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek immediate medical attention if the pain becomes unbearable despite pain relief, if the area behind your ear becomes red and tender, or if you feel dizzy and off-balance.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: Moisture trapped in the ear canal allowed environmental bacteria to grow. The ear drops contain an antibiotic to kill the bacteria and a steroid to quickly reduce the painful swelling.
Actionable Steps: Keep the ear completely dry while showering by using a cotton ball coated in petroleum jelly. Administer drops exactly as prescribed. Do not insert cotton buds.

